    "result_text": "The build is green. Let me summarize what I changed.\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaced the string membership tier with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133, cap reserving 4 for tenure).\n\n**`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` (string) response property with `membershipLevel` (integer, `int32`, min 1 / max 4). This regenerates `OwnerDto` with `membershipLevel` instead of `membershipTier`, removing the old field from the response.\n\n**`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier(Owner)` derivation with `membershipLevel(Owner)`: start at 1, `+1` when a non-blank email is present, `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3. Updated the `@Mapping` target accordingly. (The old `GOLD`/household-size branch is dropped since level no longer depends on household size.)\n\n**`src/main/java/.../rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 Updated the create audit line to record `membershipLevel={}` (via `ownerMapper.membershipLevel(owner)`) instead of the tier, and refreshed the now-stale tier comments.\n\n**Verification:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The new `coreNumericLevel` test (email present + unique names \u2192 `namesakeCount 0` \u2192 `1+1+1` capped at 3) passes, and the audit log confirms `membershipLevel=3`.\n\nA note on one judgment call: the spec says the create audit line \"recorded the tier,\" but in this codebase that line only logged id/customerCode/registrationDate \u2014 it never included the tier. Since the spec explicitly names it as a place that must record the level, I added `membershipLevel` to it. No test asserts on the audit content either way, so this is safe; let me know if you'd prefer the audit line left unchanged.",
